course: dictionary
lesson: 4495
title: One word, many meanings: haul
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words change their meaning depending on the sentence, and haul is one of them. Read the words around it to find out which meaning the author means. «The Rat grips Toad by the scruff of the neck and hauls him up into his hall» has the clues grips and scruff, so haul means to pull with great effort.
explain: Now read «Halloween haul» or «a haul of medals». There is no heavy weight to drag here. The clues are sweets and medals, so haul means an amount of something gained or caught. Always ask: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: Haul can also mean a long distance: «a long haul» is a long journey, not a load. Clues like long and journey show you which meaning is used. Look at the words together, not just the one word on its own.
fact: Haul means to pull or drag something heavy with effort.
fact: A haul is an amount of something gained or caught, such as a Halloween haul of sweets or a haul of goals.
fact: «A long haul» means a long distance or a long, hard task.
fact: Informally, to be hauled up in front of someone means to be made to go there to be told off.
fact: «The walrus uses its two long tusks to haul itself onto the ice» shows the walrus pulling its heavy body with effort.
exercise: «The Rat grips Toad by the scruff of the neck and hauls him up into his hall.» What does hauls mean here? | To sing loudly | *To pull with great effort | To sleep quietly | To give sweets away
exercise: «Maya's Halloween haul was twelve bags of sweets.» What does haul mean here? | *An amount gained | A long journey | A heavy rope | A sad song
exercise: «They had a long haul to the top of the mountain.» What does haul suggest? | A small snack | A short nap | A loud noise | *A long distance
test: «The walrus uses its tusks to haul itself onto the ice.» What does haul mean here? | To hide under the water | To sell goods to shops | To cheer loudly | *To drag with effort
test: Jayden says haul is just another word for pull, so why not use pull? Why might an author choose haul? | *It shows pulling something heavy with real effort, which is stronger than pull | It is a shorter word | It means exactly the same as pull, so there is no reason to pick it | It means happy
exam: «Ella was hauled up in front of the head teacher.» What does hauled up mean? | Lifted for a photo | *Told off | Carried upstairs | Given a prize
